#
# Build traps hit, both worth remembering:
#   1. AT_CUDA_CHECK needs <ATen/cuda/Exceptions.h>; torch/extension.h does NOT pull it in.
#   2. `pip install ninja` is NOT enough when invoking .venv/bin/python directly --
#      cpp_extension shells out to the ninja BINARY, and .venv/bin is only on PATH
#      if the venv is activated. `import ninja` succeeding proves nothing.
#   3. nvcc 12.4 (system) against torch's bundled CUDA 12.1 headers: NO conflict.
#
# --- Phase 5b: FULL TRAINING STEP vs torch SDPA (bench_train.py), 2026-09-06 ---
# Phase 5 only ever timed the forward. Training pays for both halves, and the
# backward is this kernel's weak side, so the fwd-only 2.2x was not the number
# that matters. Measured properly it is BETTER, not worse.
#
# Baseline: F.scaled_dot_product_attention, mem_efficient backend, fp16.
# Peak MB is over a complete fwd+bwd step with grads materialised.
#
# shape        caus | fwd torch/mine  x   | bwd torch/mine   x   | STEP x | peak MB
# 1,16,2048,64  no  |  4.809 / 2.005 2.40 | 43.270 / 14.081 3.07 | 2.99x | 46 -> 42
# 1,16,2048,64  YES |  2.382 / 1.123 2.12 | 22.744 /  7.511 3.03 | 2.91x | 46 -> 42
# 1,16,4096,64  no  | 15.840 / 7.249 2.18 |174.366 / 54.461 3.20 | 3.08x | 93 -> 84
# 1,16,4096,64  YES |  8.473 / 3.971 2.13 | 88.804 / 27.770 3.20 | 3.06x | 93 -> 84
#
# THE BACKWARD IS THE BIGGER WIN (3.20x) NOT THE SMALLER ONE (2.18x fwd).
# Phase 4 called the backward weak because it is 6.9x its own forward against a
# 2.5x flop ratio -- true, but torch's mem_efficient backward is 9.0x ITS own
# forward. Both are poor on Pascal; this one is less poor.
#
# Memory fix applied 2026-09-06 after the first run showed 118 MB vs torch's 93:
# the binding was allocating fp32 dQ/dK/dV then casting to fp16, holding both.
# dK/dV are written ONCE from registers, so `GOutT` now templates their storage
# type and the binding writes fp16 directly. dQ stays fp32 -- it is built by
# atomicAdd across blocks and has no choice. Result: 118 -> 84 MB, now 10%
# BELOW torch. Standalone bwd correctness bit-identical; torch-side dK/dV
# actually improved (6.7e-3 -> 3.4e-3 non-causal) by dropping a round trip.
#
# --- Usability pass, 2026-09-07: arbitrary N, GQA/MQA, d=128 in the binding ---
#
# ARBITRARY N. Padded rows/cols read as zero, then masked. A zeroed K column
# gives S=0 and exp(0-m) is NOT zero, so the tail must be masked explicitly --
# the zero fill alone silently corrupts the softmax. In the backward, Lse[row]
# and Dv[row] were read unconditionally, out of bounds for a padded row; those
# reads now route through a clamped index and dead lanes contribute exactly 0.
# Verified: 14 lengths x causal/non-causal forward, 12 lengths x grads. 0 fails.
#
# COST, and the fix. Bounds checking as a runtime branch cost 7% on the backward
# (3.06x -> 2.88x end to end). Made it a template parameter (BOUNDS) with the
# launcher picking the aligned instantiation when N % BR == 0 && N % BC == 0.
# Full speed restored: 3.11x / 2.99x. Correctness for any N, no cost when aligned.
#
# GQA/MQA. Forward remaps the K/V head per Q head. The backward could not do
# that -- dK/dV must ACCUMULATE over the query heads sharing a K/V head -- so
# blockIdx.y now indexes the K/V head and the group loop moved INSIDE, which
# keeps the accumulation in registers instead of needing atomics on dK/dV.
# Verified MHA/GQA/MQA (Hq/Hkv = 8/8, 8/4, 8/2, 8/1, 12/4, 16/2): 0 fails.
#
# d=128 IN THE BINDING -- and it overturns the Phase 3 conclusion.
# Tile shapes differ per head dim because smem binds at different points:
#   d=64  fwd 64x64 (24 KB)  bwd 64x64 (48 KB, at the cap)
#   d=128 fwd 64x32 (32 KB)  bwd 32x32 (36 KB; 64x32 would need 56 KB)
#
